  {
    "subject": "biology",
    "question": "Why does a neuron fire an action potential?",
    "firstWordsMs": 4319,
    "speech": [
      "A neuron fires when enough sodium rushes in to cross a voltage threshold.\n",
      "At rest, a neuron sits around minus seventy millivolts. When incoming signals depolarize the membrane to the threshold of minus fifty-five millivolts, voltage-gated sodium channels snap open, flooding the cell with positive ions until the peak. Potassium channels then open while sodium channels close, letting potassium rush out to reset the electrical balance."
    ],
