  {
    "subject": "chemistry",
    "question": "Why does a catalyst speed up a reaction?",
    "firstWordsMs": 4088,
    "speech": [
      "A catalyst lowers the activation energy needed for a reaction.\n",
      "Chemical reactions require reactant particles to collide with at least a minimum threshold of energy, known as activation energy, to break existing bonds and form new ones. \n\nWhen you introduce a catalyst, it offers an alternative reaction pathway that has a significantly lower energy barrier. Because the barrier is lower, a much higher fraction of colliding molecules possess enough kinetic energy to react successfully at the same temperature. The catalyst facilitates this without being consumed in the process. \n\nDoes this distinction between the reaction pathways make sense?"
    ],
