  {
    "subject": "economics",
    "question": "Why does a supply shortage raise prices?",
    "firstWordsMs": 3970,
    "speech": [
      "A shortage means fewer goods are available while demand stays the same, so buyers compete for what's left and push the price up. I'll sketch how that works.\n",
      "When the quantity supplied drops, sellers can charge more because eager buyers are willing to outbid each other to secure the limited supply. As the price climbs toward the new equilibrium, demand cools down until the market balances out again. Does that explanation make sense?"
