  {
    "subject": "computing",
    "question": "How do packets travel across a network?",
    "firstWordsMs": 4128,
    "speech": [
      "Packets are small data units that route individually through various network nodes to reach their destination.\n",
      "When you send data like a webpage or file, the network breaks it into smaller chunks called packets. Each packet carries headers containing the source and destination IP addresses, along with sequence numbers so they can be reassembled in order.\n\nRouters inspect these headers at each hop and choose the best available path across the network based on current traffic. Because of dynamic routing, different packets from the exact same file might travel along entirely different physical routes before arriving at the receiver and getting reordered.\n\nDoes that general journey from sender to receiver make sense?"
    ],
