  {
    "subject": "geography",
    "question": "Why do tectonic plates cause earthquakes?",
    "firstWordsMs": 3997,
    "speech": [
      "Earthquakes happen because plates get stuck while the rest of the Earth keeps moving, building up stress until it suddenly snaps.\n",
      "As the plates push past or grind against each other, friction keeps the contact surfaces locked in place. Even though the fault itself is stuck, the rest of the plate keeps pushing, warping and bending the surrounding rock like a loaded spring. Eventually, the built-up strain overcomes the frictional force, causing the rock to rupture and snap forward violently in what geologists call elastic rebound. That sudden release sends shockwaves rippling out through the crust, which is what we feel as an earthquake. Does this elastic rebound concept make sense?"
    ],
