      "Dramatic irony in Romeo and Juliet works by letting the audience know things the characters don't. I'll sketch how this builds tension.\n",
      "The most famous example is the climax in the Capulet tomb: we know Juliet took a sleeping potion and is about to wake up, but Romeo genuinely believes she has died. Every word of grief he speaks and every step he takes toward drinking the poison creates unbearable suspense because we see the disaster coming while he remains completely unaware. \n\nDoes this distinction between audience knowledge and character belief make sense?"
